+## Dependency graph and genuine parallelism
+
+```text
+Lane A — serialized authority and review stack
+Neon 0019 -> A1 -> A2/0020 -> B1 -> B2 -> B3 -> B4 -> B5 -> B6 -> B7 -> B8
+
+Lane B — local capture reliability
+durability plan/dev/validate -------------------------------+
+                                                            |
+Lane C — terminal completion                                v
+B8 + validated durability -> terminal PR 1 -> terminal PR 2/E2E -> MVP
+
+Post-MVP
+MVP -> visual request lane
+MVP -> external source contract -> calendar recognition -> Recall fallback
+MVP + live collaboration verification -> omnipresent agent chat
+new engine GO only -> agent-editable workspace/canvas
+```
+
+Lane A is intentionally serialized because each PR changes the security/data
+contract consumed by the next. Stacking branches for review does not permit
+out-of-order merges. Each child records its exact parent head, uses a squash-safe
+restack after the parent merges, and reruns exact-head gates.
+
+Lane B is genuinely parallel only while it owns capture-session durability,
+sequence acknowledgement, reload drain, and consent/indicator preservation. It
+must not edit PR A/B schemas, candidate behavior, finalization, or review UI.
+The issue currently lacks executable acceptance/task artifacts, so its first
+authorized action is `/plan`, not implementation.
+
+UX copy/design fixtures for B8 may be reviewed while B2-B7 run, but production
+UI implementation remains based on B7. External adapters, visual generation,
+agent chat, and canvas are not parallel MVP lanes; beginning them early would
+increase risk without shortening the critical path.
